= June 2026 — A ruling across the institutional divide

The Constitutional Tribunal led by <span class=""><b>Bogdan Święczkowski</b></span> says that **[+
president_name +]** holds greater personal authority over Supreme Court
appointments and leadership. The decision does not settle the dispute. It
deepens it: one side calls K 2/26 a constitutional judgment, the other calls
the panel itself unlawfully composed.

[? if war_top_camp = "none" and government_party != "pis": Justice Minister
**[+ justice_minister +]** says the applicant lacked standing and the ruling
has no legal effect.?][? if war_top_camp = "none" and government_party = "pis":
The PiS cabinet accepts the expansion of presidential power.?][? if
war_top_camp != "none": The
cabinet does not fight the ruling, because the man it empowers belongs to the
governing camp. Justice Minister **[+ justice_minister +]** welcomes it in one
sentence and says nothing else. Inside the camp, nobody mistakes what has
happened: a president who already believes he is more popular than his own
prime minister has been handed a power the Chancellery cannot take back.?]
[? if justice_minister_party = "Lewica": Lewica controls the ministry
that must now choose between appearing, ignoring the decision, legislating
around it or bargaining with the Palace.?][? if justice_minister_party !=
"Lewica": Lewica can press its case in Parliament and court filings, but **[+
justice_minister +]**, not the party, commands the ministry.?]

[? if war_top_camp != "none": Commentators reach for the precedent within the
hour. The first war at the top was 1990, Wałęsa against Mazowiecki. The one
this resembles is 2003: **[+ war_top_president +]** and **[+ war_top_pm +]**,
one camp, one falling government, and a presidency deciding whether to save it
or replace it.?]

