= The resignations

Every Left minister resigns the same afternoon, before the Chancellery can brief
that a reshuffle was under discussion. The letters are identical and give one
reason.

We surrender the wage enforcement we were a year from completing, the housing
programme in its second construction season, and the service guarantees that
exist because our ministry wrote them. All of it now belongs to the people who
signed them under protest, in a cabinet whose Deputy Prime Minister we left over.

Our own left says, correctly and immediately, that we should never have been
there to leave. Our own organisers say, also correctly, that the wage floor
would not exist. Both of those are now permanent features of this party.

[? if government_minority = 1: The cabinet is **[+ culture_crisis_gap +]** short
by the evening. It has not fallen and will not fall on its own. What it has to do
is find a majority — and **[+ culture_crisis_right_reserve +]** seats of it are
sitting on the right of this chamber under the name of a bloc that governed
together for eight years.?]
[? if government_minority = 0: The cabinet keeps a majority without us. Our
departure is a statement and not a lever, and everyone who wants to say so will
have said it by Monday.?]

**[+ pis_leader +]** does not ask us to reconsider. That, more than anything
said this week, tells us what was decided before Thursday.

- @pis_rupture_posture: Decide what our votes are for now

@pis_rupture_posture
title: What the Left's votes are for
subtitle: A minority cabinet has to buy every majority, and the cheapest seller is the bloc we joined to keep out.
